## Etruscan (welldigit/etruscan)

This project keeps a generated map of its codebase in a markdown vault (`.etruscan/` by default, see `config/etruscan.php`) — one note per class annotated with `#[\EtruscanNode]`, carrying its identity, description, and `## References` / `## Referenced by` links.

- **Let the map tell you which code to read.** To find a class, trace a dependency, or orient in an unfamiliar area, consult the map before crawling files — it is an index of the code, not a stand-in for it, so use it to pick the files worth opening and then open them via the note's `source` path. Descriptions are authoritative for intent and rationale; verify enforcement claims (validation, authorization, expiry) in the source before repeating them. The map is also served as MCP tools — through the `laravel-boost` server when Boost is installed, or through a separate `etruscan` server otherwise — so reach for a map tool before a broad search whenever one is available, and read `.etruscan/` directly when none is. The `etruscan-navigate` skill has the full workflow.
- **Keep the map fresh.** After changing PHP classes in scanned roots, including unannotated callers, regenerate with `php artisan etruscan:generate`, and run `php artisan etruscan:check --fresh --strict` to check source-to-note drift, scan diagnostics, exported content, vocabulary, and links. Read each tool response’s freshness status; `unknown` on a fresh checkout only means the local index is not built yet, and `etruscan:generate` rebuilds it without changing current notes. Source evidence includes unannotated callers but does not resolve dynamic Laravel wiring.
- **Never hand-edit generated blocks** (frontmatter, `## References`, `## Referenced by`) — they are overwritten on regeneration. `## Description` is the opposite: generation seeds only its empty heading, and the text is the manual, human-written part of every note — never derived from docblocks or source, never touched by regeneration, carried with its alias through any taxonomy or layout change. Write it (and further notes below the generated blocks) as a human would; an empty description is a slot waiting to be filled.
- **Put new classes on the map** with `#[\EtruscanNode('kebab-alias')]` plus axis attributes (`#[\EtruscanLayer]`, `#[\EtruscanDomain]`, `#[\EtruscanContext]`, `#[\EtruscanSlice]`) — global classes, so no import, and the leading backslash is required: without it PHP resolves the name in the current namespace and the class silently stays off the map (`etruscan:check` reports it). To annotate a whole codebase at once, use the `etruscan-annotate` skill.
